in the source tree.
.SH BUGS
A pass proves a working, encrypted, chain-verified session against
today's server configuration; it does not prove revocation checking,
which does not exist (see
.IR tls12 (2)'s
BUGS). The test needs the real network, real third-party servers,
their continued willingness to negotiate the measured suites, and a
trust bundle that still anchors their chains; none of that is under
this tree's control, so a failure here can mean the world changed
rather than the code. The hand-run corruptions above are not
automated, because they require corrupting the library itself.
.SH HISTORY
.B Tls12test
supersedes
.BR tlshellotest (1),
the disposable step-4 probe that stopped after ServerHelloDone; the
probe's hand-rolled ClientHello encoding was absorbed into
.IR tls12 (2)
and the probe deleted, exactly as its own manual promised.
